          "changeMeaning": "Introduce the difference between bonding pairs and lone pairs using a perfect tetrahedral methane molecule.",
          "narration": "Now let's examine what happens when some of the electron domains are lone pairs instead of bonding pairs. A bonding pair is held between two positive nuclei, keeping it localized. A lone pair is attracted to only one nucleus, allowing its electron cloud to spread out. Consequently, lone pairs occupy more space and exert stronger repulsion on neighboring pairs. Let's start with Methane, which has 4 bonding pairs and zero lone pairs, showing an ideal tetrahedral angle of 109.5 degrees."
        },
        {
          "clipName": "clip1_ammonia_lone_pair",
          "audioClip": "vsepr_ammonia_lone_pair",
          "autoAdvance": false,
          "changes": [
            {
              "target": "ammoniaModel",
              "enabled": true
            },
            {
              "target": "ammoniaLabelText",
              "enabled": true
            },
            {
              "target": "infoText2",
              "components": [
                {
                  "componentType": "textMeshPro",
                  "text": "1 Lone Pair (Greater Repulsion)\\nAmmonia (NH3)\\nBond Angle: 107\u00b0 (Trigonal Pyramidal)",
                  "color": [
                    1,
                    1,
                    1,
                    1
                  ],
                  "fontSize": 24,
                  "wrapText": true
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "changeMeaning": "Display Ammonia, showcasing 1 lone pair squeezing the H-N-H bond angles.",
          "narration": "Next, look at Ammonia. Like methane, its steric number is 4, so its electron-domain geometry is tetrahedral. However, it contains one lone pair, visualized here as a wider yellow cloud. This lone pair repels the three N-H bonding pairs, squeezing them closer together. This reduces the bond angle to 107 degrees and yields a Trigonal Pyramidal molecular geometry."
        },
        {
          "clipName": "clip2_water_lone_pair",
          "audioClip": "vsepr_water_lone_pair",
